==English==

===Etymology===
{{PIE root|en|h₂eyḱ}}
From {{etyl|enm|en}}, from {{etyl|dum|en}} {{m|dum|vracht}} or {{etyl|gml|en}} {{m|gml|vracht||freight money}}, ultimately from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*fra-|pos=intensive prefix}} + {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*aihtiz||possession}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{m|ine-pro|*eik'-||to possess}}. Cognate with {{cog|goh|frēht||earnings}}, {{cog|ang|ǣht||sc=Latinx|owndom}}, and a {{doublet|freight|lang=en|nocap=1}}. More at {{l|en|for-}}, {{l|en|own}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/frɔːt/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|ɔːt|lang=en}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|/frɔt/|lang=en}}
* {{a|cot-caught}} {{IPA|/frɑt/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete}} The [[hire]] of a ship or boat to transport [[cargo]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Money paid to hire a ship or boat to transport cargo; [[freight]]
#: '''''fraught''' money''.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} The [[transportation]] of goods, especially in a ship or boat.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A ship's [[cargo]], [[lading]] or [[freight]].
#: {{rfquotek|Shakespeare}}
# {{lb|en|Scotland}} A [[load]]; a [[burden]].
# {{lb|en|Scotland}} Two [[bucketfuls]] (of water).

====Derived terms====
* {{l|en|fraught-free}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete|_|except in past participle}} To [[load]] (a ship, cargo etc.).
#* '''1610''', [[s:The Tempest|The Tempest]], by Shakespeare
#*: Had I been any god of power, I would / Have sunk the sea within the earth, or e'er / It should the good ship so have swallow'd and / The '''fraughting''' souls within her.

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# {{lb|en|of a cargo-carrier}} [[laden|Laden]].
#* Shakespeare
#*: a vessel of our country richly '''fraught'''
# {{lb|en|with ''with''}} Furnished, [[equipped]].
# {{lb|en|figuratively|with ''with''}} [[loaded|Loaded]] up, [[charged]] or accompanied.
#* South
#*: a discourse '''fraught''' with all the commending excellences of speech
#* I. Taylor
#*: enterprises '''fraught''' with world-wide benefits
#* '''2005''', [[w:Plato|Plato]], ''Sophist''. Translation by Lesley Brown. [[w:Stephanus pagination|236d]].
#*: ''all these matters are '''fraught''' with paradox, just as they always have been''
# [[distressed|Distressed]].
#: ''a '''fraught''' relationship''
#* {{quote-journal|author=Oliver Brown|title=Oscar Pistorius jailed for five years – sport afforded no protection against his tragic fallibilities: Bladerunner's punishment for killing Reeva Steenkamp is but a frippery when set against the burden that her bereft parents, June and Barry, must carry &#91;print version: No room for sentimentality in this tragedy, 13 September 2014, p.&nbsp;S22&#93;|url=http://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/worldnews/oscar-pistorius/11089621/Oscar-Pistorius-jailed-for-five-years-sport-afforded-no-protection-against-his-tragic-fallibilities.html|work={{w|The Daily Telegraph}} (Sport)|date=21 October 2014|passage=But ever since the concept of "hamartia" recurred through {{w|Aristotle}}'s ''[[w:Poetics (Aristotle)|Poetics]]'', in an attempt to describe man's ingrained iniquity, our impulse has been to identify a telling defect in those brought suddenly and dramatically low. With [[w:Oscar Pistorius|[Oscar] Pistorius]], that task is '''fraught'''.}}

